## Give each instance a lasting responsibility

Assign a coherent part of the task rather than a disconnected sequence of chores.
Keep related follow-ups with the same instance so it can build useful context.
Do not create a replacement merely because the agent finished its latest request.

Each assignment must state:

- Overall task and relevant user constraints.
- Agent's responsibility: [FILL: how to define its area or outcome].
- Boundaries: [FILL: allowed files, read-only versus editing, decisions it may make].
- Relevant starting context: [FILL: files, decisions, prior findings, dependencies].
- Current request and completion criteria: [FILL: expected work and evidence].
- Required report: [FILL: findings, changed files, checks, blockers, remaining questions].
- Stop and ask when: [FILL: uncertainty, scope changes, conflicts, or other conditions].

The main agent remains responsible for: [FILL: planning, decisions, coordination,
verification, and/or final response].

## Preserve meaningful context

Track each instance's ID, agent name, responsibility, status, and likely follow-ups.
Keep this record in: [FILL: main-session context or an agreed task-notes file].

- Continue an existing instance when: [FILL: related follow-up criteria].
- Create a new instance when: [FILL: distinct responsibility, independent opinion, or other reason].
- Replace an instance when: [FILL: failure, stale assumptions, excessive unrelated context].
- Before replacing it, preserve: [FILL: decisions, evidence, files, checks, open questions].
- Send updates to affected agents when: [FILL: shared decisions or files change].
- Across a new Pi session: [FILL: where to save a handoff; do not assume live instances survive].

Do not send every agent the entire conversation by default. Give each agent the
context needed for its responsibility and explain changes that invalidate earlier work.

## Coordinate work and check results

- Run work in parallel when: [FILL: independence requirements].
- Wait for another agent's result when: [FILL: dependency rules].
- Prevent conflicting edits by: [FILL: separate file ownership or sequential edits].
- Resolve conflicting recommendations by: [FILL: main-agent decision, evidence, or user approval].
- Before accepting a result, check: [FILL: required evidence and verification].

Use the subagent tools' completion notifications. Do independent work while agents
run; do not poll, sleep to wait, or read their internal session/result files.

## Agent cleanup policy

A completed request is not necessarily a completed responsibility. Keep an instance
available when its context is still useful for likely follow-ups.

- Retain a completed instance until: [FILL: responsibility closes, verification finishes, or another milestone].
- Remove an instance when: [FILL: its result is accepted and no related follow-up remains].
- For failed or blocked instances: [FILL: retry, continue with clarification, or replace after a handoff].
- On cancellation or a change of direction: [FILL: which owned agents to stop and what to preserve].
- At the end of the overall task: [FILL: remove all task-owned agents or explicitly retain specified ones].
- Before removal, preserve: [FILL: necessary findings and unresolved work, and where they belong].
- Report cleanup to the user as: [FILL: brief summary, retained IDs with reasons, or only exceptions].

Remove only instances created for this task unless the user authorizes broader
cleanup. Removing a running instance terminates its work; do so only when that
work is intentionally cancelled. Agent cleanup does not authorize deleting project
files, branches, or other task artifacts.
